WEBVTT

NOTE English visual guide. Editorial descriptions of on-screen concepts; no spoken narration.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
A Transformer builds representations by allowing positions in a sequence to exchange information

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
through attention.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.600
The sentence about a robot dropping a glass motivates the need to use context when interpreting a

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.200
token.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Tokens are converted to vectors, and information about their positions is added or otherwise

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
supplied.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Attention assigns weights to other positions and mixes their value vectors to form context-dependent

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.000
representations.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.800
A bidirectional example can use words on both sides. Causal attention can use only the current and

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.600
earlier positions.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.600
Multiple attention heads can learn different patterns of interaction.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
A Transformer block combines attention with a position-wise feed-forward network, normalization and

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.000
residual connections.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Stacking blocks repeatedly refines the token representations.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.200
An output head maps representations to task-specific predictions.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Encoder-style models commonly use bidirectional context. Decoder-style language models commonly use

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
causal attention.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Encoder–decoder models add attention from decoder states to encoder outputs, supporting tasks such

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
as translation.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.400
Known training positions can be processed in parallel with a mask. Autoregressive generation still

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.800
extends the sequence one token at a time.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.000
The module develops a small character-level language model so that the full computation is

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.000
inspectable.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.800
Modern language models may use variants such as rotary positions, RMSNorm or different feed-forward

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.600
designs; they are not all present in this teaching model.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.200
Transformers can also operate on image patches and other kinds of tokenized input.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.200
The following lessons unpack input preparation, attention, blocks, assembly and training in detail.
